As usual, I think it's a combination of factors. 

Product aging is an issue: 
1) The Model 3 is an aged model by now; 
2) The Model Y refresh was a very light refresh (side view is nearly identical with old one); 
3) No meaningful improvement in battery tech. Many vehicles now charge much faster (Tesla used to be among the fastest charging);
4) The in-car infotainment gap is closing: other brands offer systems which are comparable. Perhaps not quite as good but barely distinguishable to the average consumer; 
5) Tesla opening its charging network, which used to be a key selling point, together with substantially larger non-Tesla charging infrastructure mostly/partially removed this sales drivers. 

Competition is clearly catching up since Tesla made little progress in the last few years. 

A few years ago, the gap between the Model Y to every other BEV SUV was large. Now, that gap is all but gone relative to top available models: their infotainment is on par, they charge faster, have more range, have nicer interiors/more features, and sell at similar or lower price points. 

FSD is the only relevant delta but can't be used in many markets yet. Tesla needs FSD unlock ASAP. 

Then, of course, there is the political stuff. That's obviously also no helpful. There is a segment of the population that's been alienated and won't buy the brand. Depending on where you live, some people may still remember the Tesla vandalism. 

My prediction is that we won't see Tesla return to over 10-11% of EV market share. The increasing breadth of the market makes this also near impossible with no model lineup expansion in which Elon didn't want to invest for valuation reasons. Even if Tesla sold 500k more vehicles, it wouldn't make enough of a difference. Hence, he made the right call and focused resources on FSD, CC and Optimus.

